      "output": {
        "message": "Freezing usually happens when studying focuses on recognizing material rather than generating it from scratch under time pressure. Two adjustments can help bridge that gap: 1. **Practice timed retrieval, not just reading.** After a chapter, close the book and give yourself five minutes to answer a prompt or summarize the core concept in writing. Getting used to pulling the information out of your head without notes makes a big difference on test day.\n2. **Jot down raw bullet points first.** When you see a written prompt on an exam, immediately write two or three key terms, dates, or formulas in the margin before trying to form complete sentences. Having raw notes on the page breaks the blank-page freeze and gives you an outline to follow. Do you have practice essay or short-answer prompts available for this class, or do you need to create your own?",
