        "message": "Three weeks is solid time to train specifically for written retrieval. A straightforward way to break down those three weeks: - **Week 1: Build a prompt bank and practice outlining.** Turn textbook headings, end-of-chapter summaries, or past quizzes into written questions. Practice writing just 3–4 core bullet points for each prompt within two minutes, book closed. - **Week 2: Timed, closed-book writing.** Pick 2–3 prompts a day. Set a strict timer matching the real exam pace (e.g., 8–10 minutes per answer) and write full responses without notes. The goal is desensitizing yourself to the timer and building the habit of starting immediately.\n- **Week 3: Full test simulations.** Sit down for at least one or two timed mock exams using mixed questions under test conditions—no notes, quiet room, strict time limit. Review where your recall lagged and brush up on those specific spots. If you have a specific chapter or topic you want to turn into sample written prompts, we can draft a few now.",
